  "live_records": {
    "q-105784": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105784",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(6),$ what is $P(X<4)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(6).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8Γ(3)}{x}^{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(3)=2!=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
